1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,980
We'll be right back.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:59,980
We'll be right back.

3
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,820
I restrained myself and gave him my favorite parable because I felt like that was a loving and gracious and sacrificial thing to do.

4
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:13,520
So, if you would, give a warm welcome to Sam Rogers.

5
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:16,740
He is the pastor of Hope Valley House Churches.

6
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:17,420
Yeah, come on up.

7
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:27,820
He's been here a time or two in the past, and I have referred to him as my pastor because every pastor should have a pastor.

8
00:01:27,820 --> 00:01:30,540
And so, I'm grateful that you're back.

9
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:34,380
I'm grateful to hear what the Lord has prepared for us through you.

10
00:01:34,380 --> 00:01:35,540
Thanks for being here.

11
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:36,080
Thank you.

12
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:39,240
Well, good morning, everyone.

13
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:40,280
How are we all doing?

14
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:41,420
Good?

15
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:47,180
Can I tell you, I've been wearing glasses for only about a year now, and I cannot keep these things clean.

16
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:54,040
So, if I look cross-eyed, it's because I have glares on them, and I'm just trying to make eye conduct as best as I can.

17
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:54,740
Is that all right?

18
00:01:54,740 --> 00:01:55,200
Okay.

19
00:01:56,180 --> 00:02:03,680
Well, I am very excited to be here and very humbled by the offer and the time to be with you guys.

20
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:10,380
And I was pretty excited when Ryan had told me about the series and asked me to come preach on the parables.

21
00:02:10,380 --> 00:02:14,740
On the inside, I was like, let it be the one about the prodigal son, although we'll talk about that name.

22
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:20,380
There's a better name for it, but I was trying to keep it, like, low-key.

23
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:23,500
Like, I don't want to, you know, like, if you want that, but he gave it to me.

24
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:26,600
So, it worked out.

25
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:29,180
And I've actually listened.

26
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:35,340
I've taken the time to listen through the last four messages from Kent and Corey and the last two that you did.

27
00:02:35,340 --> 00:02:39,580
And I know you guys have been talking about just the meaning of the parables and why Jesus taught in parables.

28
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:48,920
And I think that there's something about what has already been said that is especially important for us to think about today as we listen, right?

29
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:56,680
Because the parables, what they do is they both reveal truth to those who are receptive, right?

30
00:02:56,680 --> 00:03:02,720
They reveal truth to those who are receptive, but they actually conceal truth to those who are hard-hearted.

31
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:07,300
And we see Jesus say this essentially in Matthew 13, 11 through 13, right?

32
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:19,300
And so, the parables both reveal and judge depending on the heart condition of the listener, right?

33
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:26,420
In other words, I believe Kent was talking about the way that they're crafting a way that they stir up curiosity, right?

34
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:32,220
And they do, but they can't do that if you're not ready to be curious is one way to think about it.

35
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:38,180
So, one of my favorite lines that Paul has in his letter to the Corinthians is he says,

36
00:03:38,180 --> 00:03:46,800
anyone who thinks they know, anyone who thinks they understand does not yet understand in the way that they ought to, right?

37
00:03:46,980 --> 00:03:51,840
So, if we come to the Lord and we're like, I got this, I understand these aspects of God's character,

38
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:58,420
then we can actually hear the parables and not understand them at all because we're not ready to be curious.

39
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:01,680
And so, we're not presenting it to be stirred up.

40
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:02,280
Does that make sense?

41
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:05,340
So, it depends on the condition of the heart.

42
00:04:05,340 --> 00:04:11,880
So, today, we want to be people who are ready to receive and to hear what the Lord is saying

43
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:14,980
because this is what I want us to understand when we go through this parable.

44
00:04:14,980 --> 00:04:26,080
The love of the Father, the love of God for us, must be first received in order to be understood.

45
00:04:26,080 --> 00:04:28,960
We'll talk about that some more.

46
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:30,860
Sometimes we have that backwards, right?

47
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:33,360
I can receive it once I understand it.

48
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:34,600
No, wrong.

49
00:04:35,820 --> 00:04:38,980
You'll understand it once you've received it, right?

50
00:04:38,980 --> 00:04:45,580
And so, we have to have an open heart posture today in order to really grasp, I think, what Jesus is revealing through this parable.

51
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:50,720
And so, the parable comes actually in response to the first two verses of Matthew 15.

52
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:53,000
And I'll read these quickly because it says, like,

53
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:57,300
the tax collectors and the sinners, they were approaching Jesus to listen to him.

54
00:04:57,300 --> 00:05:01,280
And the Pharisees and the scribes were complaining, right?

55
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:05,080
This man, Jesus, welcomes sinners and he eats with them.

56
00:05:05,340 --> 00:05:12,700
And so, Jesus goes on and he tells two quick short parables, the lost sheep and the lost coin.

57
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:14,300
If you're familiar with those.

58
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:19,020
But then he goes on to talk about a father and two sons.

59
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:20,440
That's what we're going to focus on this morning.

60
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:24,220
And really, I'm just going to say it bluntly like this.

61
00:05:24,220 --> 00:05:30,080
These two sons represent two different groups of people in this room.

62
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:32,320
Okay?

63
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:35,400
Everyone in this room is going to be one of these two sons.

64
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:40,860
And so, these two sons represent all of us and we fall into one of those two categories.

65
00:05:40,860 --> 00:05:42,060
And so, we want to listen that way.

66
00:05:43,340 --> 00:05:44,860
So, we're going to go ahead and read.

67
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:48,480
We're going to read Matthew 15, 11 to 32.

68
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:52,380
Just going to read straight through and then we're going to kind of unpack it together.

69
00:05:52,380 --> 00:05:52,880
How's it sound?

70
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:53,480
Good?

71
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:54,360
You guys ready?

72
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:54,840
All right.

73
00:05:54,840 --> 00:05:57,360
So, in verse 11, it says,

74
00:05:57,360 --> 00:06:23,720
And not many days later, the younger son gathered together all he had and traveled to a distant country where he squandered his estate and foolish living.

75
00:06:24,580 --> 00:06:32,080
And after he had spent everything, a severe famine struck that country and he had nothing.

76
00:06:32,080 --> 00:06:40,180
And then he went to work for one of the citizens of that country who sent him into the fields to feed pigs.

77
00:06:40,180 --> 00:06:49,800
And he longed to eat his fill from the pods that the pigs were eating, but no one would give him anything.

78
00:06:52,220 --> 00:06:54,240
And when he came to his senses, he said,

79
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:58,220
How many of my father's hired workers have more than enough food?

80
00:06:58,220 --> 00:07:00,380
And here I am dying of hunger.

81
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:01,420
I'll get up.

82
00:07:01,420 --> 00:07:03,340
I'll go to my father and say to him,

83
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:06,960
Father, I've sinned against heaven and in your sight.

84
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:09,380
I'm no longer worthy to be called your son.

85
00:07:09,380 --> 00:07:11,540
Make me like one of your hired workers.

86
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:20,220
So, he got up and he went back to his father.

87
00:07:20,220 --> 00:07:29,300
And while the son was still a long way off, his father saw him and was filled with compassion.

88
00:07:29,300 --> 00:07:33,140
And he ran and he threw his arms around his neck and he kissed him.

89
00:07:33,140 --> 00:07:37,060
And the son said to him, Father, I've sinned against heaven and in your sight.

90
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:40,120
I'm no longer worthy to be called your son.

91
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:43,760
But the father told his servants, quick, bring out the best robe.

92
00:07:43,760 --> 00:07:45,000
Put it on him.

93
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,420
Put a ring on his finger and sandals on his feet.

94
00:07:47,420 --> 00:07:50,140
And then bring the fattened calf and slaughter it.

95
00:07:50,140 --> 00:07:51,640
And let's celebrate with a feast.

96
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:54,880
Because the son of mine was dead.

97
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:57,760
And he's alive again.

98
00:07:58,240 --> 00:07:59,280
He was lost.

99
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:00,620
And he's found.

100
00:08:00,620 --> 00:08:02,940
And so, they began to celebrate.

101
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:06,920
Now, his older son was in the field.

102
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:11,580
And as he came near the house, he heard music and dancing.

103
00:08:11,580 --> 00:08:16,520
And so, he summoned one of the servants, questioning what these things meant.

104
00:08:16,520 --> 00:08:19,780
Your brother is here, he was told.

105
00:08:19,780 --> 00:08:26,580
And your father has slaughtered the fattened calf because he has him back safe and sound.

106
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:28,160
Then he became angry.

107
00:08:28,160 --> 00:08:33,380
That's part of response, right?

108
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:36,380
To your brother coming back home.

109
00:08:36,380 --> 00:08:38,020
He became angry.

110
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:41,240
And he didn't want to go in.

111
00:08:41,240 --> 00:08:43,800
So, his father came out and pleaded with him.

112
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:48,560
But he replied to his father, look, I have been slaving many years for you.

113
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:53,420
And I have never disobeyed your orders.

114
00:08:53,420 --> 00:08:56,840
Yet, you never gave me a goat.

115
00:08:56,960 --> 00:09:01,460
So, that I could celebrate with my friends.

116
00:09:01,460 --> 00:09:08,960
But when this son of yours came, who has devoured your assets with prostitutes, you slaughtered the fattened calf for him.

117
00:09:08,960 --> 00:09:15,120
Son, he said to him, you are always with me.

118
00:09:15,260 --> 00:09:17,860
And everything I have is yours.

119
00:09:17,860 --> 00:09:23,680
But we had to celebrate and rejoice because this brother of yours was dead.

120
00:09:23,680 --> 00:09:26,120
And he's alive again.

121
00:09:26,120 --> 00:09:28,760
He was lost and he's found.

122
00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:40,520
So, first thing we want to think about as we really unpack this parable is we want to consider the father's love.

123
00:09:40,680 --> 00:09:44,840
Which is on display here really through this whole parable.

124
00:09:44,840 --> 00:09:48,340
This parable is really a parable about the father's love.

125
00:09:48,340 --> 00:09:49,380
Right?

126
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:55,200
The younger son only shows hate to his father.

127
00:09:56,140 --> 00:10:01,400
And in order to understand this, we have to get out of, I know we've been talking about this a lot already in the series.

128
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:11,560
We have to get out of our cultural context and get into the Middle Eastern context of the time to understand, let's say, the depth and the gravity of what the younger son had asked for.

129
00:10:11,640 --> 00:10:18,140
Even in our culture, like asking for your inheritance before your parents are dead is like, you know, it's tacky.

130
00:10:18,140 --> 00:10:19,720
Right?

131
00:10:19,720 --> 00:10:25,940
Like if we all heard that happen, we'd be like, ooh, not cool, man.

132
00:10:25,940 --> 00:10:27,180
Not cool.

133
00:10:27,180 --> 00:10:33,720
But we would pretty much just understand it as mildly offensive, you know, and tacky.

134
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:34,320
Right?

135
00:10:35,840 --> 00:10:42,160
In their culture, this request was a public humiliation to his father.

136
00:10:42,160 --> 00:10:50,540
Asking for what your father was going to, you know, the inheritance you were going to receive from your father.

137
00:10:50,540 --> 00:10:57,540
Asking for that while he was still alive was saying the following things.

138
00:10:57,540 --> 00:11:00,980
It was saying, you're already dead to me.

139
00:11:00,980 --> 00:11:03,260
Right?

140
00:11:04,660 --> 00:11:06,960
I want nothing to do with you.

141
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:12,700
I'm abandoning my place in the community.

142
00:11:12,700 --> 00:11:17,940
Because by the way, remember, we have a very individualized culture in Western America.

143
00:11:17,940 --> 00:11:21,120
This is not the culture of the time in the Middle East.

144
00:11:21,120 --> 00:11:21,960
It still isn't.

145
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:22,200
Right?

146
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:26,240
Eastern culture is way more community focused.

147
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:26,520
Right?

148
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:29,260
Very little individualism as we would know it.

149
00:11:29,260 --> 00:11:29,640
Right?

150
00:11:29,640 --> 00:11:32,900
And so when the son does this, it's shaming the father.

151
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:34,480
It's shaming the family.

152
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:38,260
It's the son basically offending the entire community.

153
00:11:38,260 --> 00:11:40,580
We'll come back to that in a minute.

154
00:11:43,020 --> 00:11:47,860
This is a deeply hateful thing the younger son is doing.

155
00:11:47,860 --> 00:11:48,740
Right?

156
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:52,500
He's effectively wishing his father dead.

157
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:54,840
And he's saying, I don't have a place in this community.

158
00:11:54,840 --> 00:11:58,100
It would be like disowning his own family name.

159
00:11:58,100 --> 00:12:02,580
It'd be like leaving his hometown and then claiming to have no hometown.

160
00:12:02,580 --> 00:12:04,700
I don't even know who those people are.

161
00:12:04,700 --> 00:12:04,940
Right?

162
00:12:05,140 --> 00:12:06,540
He's cutting himself off entirely.

163
00:12:06,540 --> 00:12:07,240
He's cutting himself off entirely.

164
00:12:07,240 --> 00:12:09,080
It's deeply hurtful.

165
00:12:09,080 --> 00:12:10,120
It's deeply offensive.

166
00:12:10,120 --> 00:12:10,660
Right?

167
00:12:10,660 --> 00:12:19,260
In this culture, more than likely, a son even asking for this could result in a son being put to death by his family.

168
00:12:21,420 --> 00:12:25,800
Just to help us kind of grab the gravity of what the younger son is doing here.

169
00:12:25,800 --> 00:12:26,140
Right?

170
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:33,580
So, actually, what Jesus' listeners would have expected was, oh, and then the younger son died.

171
00:12:36,360 --> 00:12:43,540
And so, to everybody's surprise, the father actually does it and gives him, which, by the way, would have been a tremendous amount of work.

172
00:12:43,540 --> 00:12:45,440
They have, like, a bank account, like, hey, here's your portion.

173
00:12:45,440 --> 00:12:49,860
He would have literally given him livestock and property and all these things.

174
00:12:49,860 --> 00:12:53,640
The younger son would have had to sell all that stuff, which would have been an even greater offense.

175
00:12:53,640 --> 00:12:58,360
And then he goes and just wastes it on a party trip, essentially.

176
00:13:00,980 --> 00:13:05,520
And so, he squandered everything his father had worked hard to provide.

177
00:13:05,520 --> 00:13:10,760
And not just things that his father had worked hard to provide, but that his grandfather had worked hard to provide.

178
00:13:10,760 --> 00:13:11,740
And it's great, great.

179
00:13:11,740 --> 00:13:15,960
Are we grabbing, like, the severity of this request from the younger son?

180
00:13:15,960 --> 00:13:20,120
It is a dark thing this boy is doing.

181
00:13:20,120 --> 00:13:21,100
Right?

182
00:13:21,100 --> 00:13:27,600
So, again, understanding that what they would have expected would have been that the father would have killed him.

183
00:13:27,600 --> 00:13:29,840
And the community would have joined in.

184
00:13:29,960 --> 00:13:32,860
And they would have all been like, we feel absolutely great about this decision.

185
00:13:32,860 --> 00:13:35,000
Let's go home.

186
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:36,180
We're going to sleep like babies tonight.

187
00:13:36,180 --> 00:13:37,000
No remorse.

188
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:37,420
Right?

189
00:13:37,420 --> 00:13:39,980
Like, they would have been totally okay with this choice.

190
00:13:39,980 --> 00:13:40,480
Right?

191
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:46,160
And yet, what we see is that the father actually only shows love for his son.

192
00:13:46,160 --> 00:13:52,040
First, by actually granting the request, which is incredible.

193
00:13:52,040 --> 00:13:54,100
Right?

194
00:13:55,620 --> 00:14:06,380
But then, when the younger son comes to his senses and he comes home, it just makes the point of saying, he wasn't, like, close by.

195
00:14:06,380 --> 00:14:11,320
He was, like, on the horizon.

196
00:14:13,960 --> 00:14:15,880
And the father runs to him.

197
00:14:15,880 --> 00:14:17,760
Here's another thing to understand about that culture.

198
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:21,400
The patriarch of the family doesn't run.

199
00:14:21,400 --> 00:14:23,180
They don't run, guys.

200
00:14:23,180 --> 00:14:25,260
They don't.

201
00:14:25,260 --> 00:14:31,940
In that culture, the father would have made himself a laughing stock to the community.

202
00:14:33,100 --> 00:14:39,120
Just by running, it would have been seen as so personally humiliating.

203
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:43,100
Father doesn't care.

204
00:14:43,100 --> 00:14:46,300
He runs to him.

205
00:14:48,060 --> 00:14:55,640
He publicly humiliates himself in order to reconcile with his son and restore him.

206
00:14:55,640 --> 00:15:07,260
You know, exactly the way that Jesus publicly humiliated himself to restore and reconcile you and me.

207
00:15:10,840 --> 00:15:14,220
And so, the father humiliates himself, and he goes further.

208
00:15:14,220 --> 00:15:16,000
He immediately restores.

209
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,380
This is why the father is moving quick.

210
00:15:18,380 --> 00:15:19,240
Right?

211
00:15:19,240 --> 00:15:25,120
He's like, before anyone has a chance to raise an issue with what I'm doing, quick.

212
00:15:25,120 --> 00:15:26,700
Grab the robe.

213
00:15:26,700 --> 00:15:27,400
Grab the ring.

214
00:15:27,400 --> 00:15:28,740
Get the dinner ready.

215
00:15:28,740 --> 00:15:29,820
We're moving on this.

216
00:15:29,820 --> 00:15:33,580
Because I don't want to delay at all.

217
00:15:33,580 --> 00:15:40,020
Reconciling my son, not only to me, but back to the whole community.

218
00:15:40,020 --> 00:15:42,200
The first, think about the picture, guys.

219
00:15:42,200 --> 00:15:46,600
The first time the community sees the son again, he's wearing the robe.

220
00:15:46,600 --> 00:15:48,240
He's wearing the ring.

221
00:15:48,240 --> 00:15:49,620
And he's like the head of the table.

222
00:15:49,620 --> 00:15:52,740
This is the father's action, right?

223
00:15:52,740 --> 00:16:02,800
So, the father immediately restores the son, not only to himself, but uses his position to restore him to the whole community.

224
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:08,560
Kind of like how Jesus grabbed us out of the clutches of hell and placed us in his church.

225
00:16:09,200 --> 00:16:10,120
Right?

226
00:16:10,120 --> 00:16:15,400
And so, he replaces isolation with relationship.

227
00:16:15,400 --> 00:16:18,360
He restores his name.

228
00:16:18,360 --> 00:16:20,420
He restores his position.

229
00:16:20,420 --> 00:16:22,220
He restores his place.

230
00:16:22,220 --> 00:16:24,680
He makes him belong again.

231
00:16:24,680 --> 00:16:25,660
Right?

232
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:27,240
We have to understand that culturally.

233
00:16:27,240 --> 00:16:31,380
This wasn't just like a, hey, he said he's sorry, he came back, let's have a party.

234
00:16:31,380 --> 00:16:38,360
This is the father moving quickly to protect his son from the son's own choices.

235
00:16:40,300 --> 00:16:41,560
And the community effect they had.

236
00:16:41,560 --> 00:16:42,660
